{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"6c2b4dbc047e3ada","type":"text","text":"# Question 1\nWhat is a modern approach to calculating intraband and interband transitions properly? Specifically emission and emission under illumination - photoluminescence.","x":-355,"y":200,"width":335,"height":220,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"context_abstract","type":"text","text":"# Context: Metal Photoluminescence\n\n**Summary:**\nThis work quantifies the contribution of **interband transitions** (conduction to valence band) to the photoluminescence of Gold under external illumination. We extend previous theoretical frameworks—originally limited to intraband transitions—to include interband channels under both continuous-wave and transient excitation. Our approach combines rigorous numerical simulations with analytical validations.","x":-642,"y":-200,"width":602,"height":280},
		{"id":"4280441fd574bee9","type":"text","text":"# Instructions\n1. Give answer to a question node by creating an answer node (an arrow should point from question to answer to show causality)\n2. Answers should generally have the context of the all the tree history leading to it. That means that each answer should be given after having read the entires relevant context leading to the seed node.\n3. Answers should be given in full rigor and comprehension emphasizing math as much as needed to make the point clear.\n4. Math formatting should be Mathjax with $ and \\$$  environments.","x":160,"y":-200,"width":450,"height":400},
		{"id":"98c0ac70a902812f","type":"text","text":"# Question 2\n\nWhat is Photoluminescence? How does it differ from reflection or thermal emission on a fundamental microscopic basis?\n","x":-1040,"y":-151,"width":292,"height":183,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"d2a9f0e51c7b4a6d","type":"text","text":"# Answer 2\n\nPhotoluminescence (PL) is **radiative recombination after optical excitation**. Microscopically, light first drives the electronic system out of equilibrium (changes occupations and possibly coherences), and then a subset of electronic transitions emits photons.\n\nA compact emission-rate form is\n$$\nI_{\\mathrm{PL}}(\\omega) \\propto \\rho_{\\mathrm{ph}}(\\omega)\\sum_{n,m}\\iint_{\\mathrm{BZ}} |M_{nm}(\\mathbf{k}_1,\\mathbf{k}_2)|^2\\, f_n(\\mathbf{k}_1)\\,[1-f_m(\\mathbf{k}_2)]\\,\\delta\\!\\left(E_n(\\mathbf{k}_1)-E_m(\\mathbf{k}_2)-\\hbar\\omega\\right)\\,d^3k_1\\,d^3k_2.\n$$\nThis is exactly the FGR-style picture used in your framework: matrix element, occupation/vacancy factors, and energy conservation.\n\nFor metals in your project, the sum separates into\n$$\nI_{\\mathrm{PL}}(\\omega)=I_{\\mathrm{cc}}(\\omega)+I_{\\mathrm{cv}}(\\omega),\n$$\nwith intraband $\\mathrm{cc}$ and interband $\\mathrm{cv}$ channels.\n\n## How PL differs fundamentally from reflection\n\nReflection is primarily an **elastic, coherent scattering** process of the incident optical field by induced polarization/current at the *same* optical frequency:\n$$\n\\mathbf{P}(\\omega)=\\varepsilon_0\\chi(\\omega)\\mathbf{E}_{\\mathrm{inc}}(\\omega),\\qquad\nE_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\omega)=r(\\omega)E_{\\mathrm{inc}}(\\omega),\\qquad R=|r|^2.\n$$\nKey microscopic distinction:\n- Reflection is encoded by linear response ($\\chi$, $\\varepsilon$, boundary conditions), not by a recombination phase-space factor $f(1-f)$ for emitted photons at shifted energies.\n- In ideal specular reflection, photons keep frequency and phase relation to the pump ($\\omega_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\omega_{\\mathrm{in}}$).\n- PL is generally inelastic and mostly incoherent relative to the pump; emitted photons are distributed spectrally by electronic transition phase space.\n\n## How PL differs fundamentally from thermal emission\n\nThermal emission is **equilibrium spontaneous emission** from thermally populated fluctuations. Macroscopically:\n$$\nI_{\\mathrm{th}}(\\omega,T)=\\varepsilon(\\omega,T)\\,B_{\\omega}(T),\n$$\nwith Planck spectrum\n$$\nB_{\\omega}(T)=\\frac{\\hbar\\omega^3}{4\\pi^3 c^2}\\frac{1}{e^{\\hbar\\omega/k_B T}-1}.\n$$\nMicroscopically, thermal radiation is fixed by equilibrium fluctuation-dissipation (or detailed balance / Kirchhoff), i.e. occupations are thermal $f^T$ and the spectrum is temperature-controlled.\n\nPL instead is driven by a **non-equilibrium** carrier distribution produced by illumination:\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{k})=f^T(\\mathbf{k})+\\delta f_{\\mathrm{pump}}(\\mathbf{k}),\n$$\nand $\\delta f_{\\mathrm{pump}}$ reshapes $I_{\\mathrm{PL}}$ beyond pure Planckian form.\n\n## Practical decomposition in your experiments\n\nA measured spectrum near pumped gold is usefully written as\n$$\nI_{\\mathrm{meas}}(\\omega)=I_{\\mathrm{refl}}\\,\\delta(\\omega-\\omega_L)+I_{\\mathrm{PL}}(\\omega;\\delta f)+I_{\\mathrm{th}}(\\omega;T_e,T_l).\n$$\nInterpretation:\n- $I_{\\mathrm{refl}}$: coherent elastic channel at the laser frequency $\\omega_L$.\n- $I_{\\mathrm{PL}}$: non-equilibrium radiative electronic transitions (intraband + interband).\n- $I_{\\mathrm{th}}$: equilibrium-like thermal background set by effective temperature and emissivity.\n\nSo the most fundamental microscopic discriminator is:\n- **Reflection:** field scattering (coherent, elastic, no recombination phase-space integral).\n- **Thermal emission:** equilibrium fluctuation radiation (Planck/Kirchhoff constrained).\n- **Photoluminescence:** pump-generated non-equilibrium electronic recombination radiation (FGR phase-space constrained).\n\nInternal anchors: [[1 - Introduction]], [[2.1 - General Emission Integral]], [[A9 - Kirchhoff's Law Of Radiation]], [[Abstract]].\n","x":-1200,"y":187,"width":686,"height":980},
		{"id":"b349f391fae6ccaf","type":"text","text":"# Question 2.1\n\nI am interested in how reflection is treated on a microscopic level, similar to how we treat photoluminescence using Fermi's golden rule.","x":-1892,"y":187,"width":364,"height":184,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"5776a2878f4af774","type":"text","text":"# Question 2.1.1\n\nDoes this connect somehow to Heisenberg-Kramers model?\n","x":-1864,"y":1842,"width":309,"height":120,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"f3ab92de4c76185b","type":"text","text":"# Answer 2.1.1\n\nYes. If by \"Heisenberg-Kramers\" you mean the **Kramers-Heisenberg dispersion formula**, it is directly connected to microscopic reflection.[^1][^2]\n\nAt the amplitude level, light scattering is second-order in the light-matter interaction:\n$$\n\\mathcal{M}_{fi}(\\omega_i,\\omega_s)=\\sum_n\\left[\\frac{\\langle f|\\hat D_s^{\\dagger}|n\\rangle\\langle n|\\hat D_i|i\\rangle}{E_i+\\hbar\\omega_i-E_n+i\\Gamma_n/2}+\\frac{\\langle f|\\hat D_i|n\\rangle\\langle n|\\hat D_s^{\\dagger}|i\\rangle}{E_i-\\hbar\\omega_s-E_n+i0^+}\\right].\n$$\nThe scattering rate is\n$$\n\\frac{dW}{d\\Omega\\,d\\omega_s}\\propto |\\mathcal{M}_{fi}|^2\\,\\delta(E_f+\\hbar\\omega_s-E_i-\\hbar\\omega_i).\n$$\nThis is the Kramers-Heisenberg framework.[^1][^2]\n\nFor **reflection** (elastic/Rayleigh channel), we take $f=i$ and $\\omega_s\\approx\\omega_i$, then coherently sum over many emitters in the medium. In linear response this becomes the susceptibility/conductivity description:\n$$\nJ_i(\\omega)=\\sum_j\\sigma_{ij}(\\omega)E_j(\\omega),\\qquad\n\\sigma_{ij}(\\omega)=\\frac{i}{\\omega+i0^+}\\left[\\Pi^R_{ij}(\\omega)-\\Pi^R_{ij}(0)\\right],\n$$\n$$\n\\varepsilon(\\omega)=1+\\frac{i\\sigma(\\omega)}{\\varepsilon_0\\omega},\\qquad\nr(\\omega)=\\frac{1-\\sqrt{\\varepsilon(\\omega)}}{1+\\sqrt{\\varepsilon(\\omega)}}.\n$$\nSo the practical route is\n$$\n\\text{Kramers-Heisenberg (microscopic scattering)}\\;\\to\\;\\chi,\\sigma,\\varepsilon\\;\\to\\;\\text{Fresnel }R.\n$$\n\nThis is also why the **Kramers-Kronig** structure appears: causality of the same response function links dispersive and absorptive parts.[^3][^4]\n\nCompared with your PL treatment:\n- PL keeps explicit population factors $f(1-f)$ and inelastic energy transfer.\n- Reflection in linear optics is dominated by coherent elastic response encoded in $\\chi(\\omega)$ / $\\varepsilon(\\omega)$.\n- Under strong pumping, reflection can become nonlinear, and then one moves beyond simple linear Kramers-Heisenberg/Fresnel to nonequilibrium response formalisms.[^2][^5]\n\nSupplemental internal anchors: [[2.1 - General Emission Integral]], [[1 - Introduction]], [[A9 - Kirchhoff's Law Of Radiation]].\n\n[^1]: H. A. Kramers and W. Heisenberg, *Uber die Streuung von Strahlung durch Atome*, Z. Phys. **31**, 681-708 (1925). [https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02980624](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02980624)\n[^2]: L. J. P. Ament, M. van Veenendaal, T. P. Devereaux, J. P. Hill, and J. van den Brink, *Resonant inelastic x-ray scattering studies of elementary excitations*, Rev. Mod. Phys. **83**, 705 (2011). [https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.83.705](https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.83.705)\n[^3]: R. Kubo, *Statistical-Mechanical Theory of Irreversible Processes. I*, J. Phys. Soc. Jpn. **12**, 570 (1957). [https://doi.org/10.1143/JPSJ.12.570](https://doi.org/10.1143/JPSJ.12.570)\n[^4]: M. Dressel and G. Gruner, *Electrodynamics of Solids* (Cambridge Univ. Press, 2002). [https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511606168](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511606168)\n[^5]: M. Born and E. Wolf, *Principles of Optics* (7th ed.). [https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108769914](https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108769914)\n","x":-2089,"y":2022,"width":760,"height":1300},
		{"id":"6fca91582d3d93bd","type":"text","text":"# Microscopic Specularity\n\nYou say that reflection is a coherent process. I wonder if on the microscopic level, a photon that meets a planar interface is reflected at a specular angle 100% of the time. Is it that quantum mechanically it is very likely that the photon scatters in the same direction, but in practice it is capable of reflecting in another angle only with lesser probability? Namely, if I were to shoot a photon at a mirror in a certain angle and put a detector in a slightly shifted angle from specular reflection I would measure every once in a while a photon reflecting in that direction?","x":-2735,"y":1080,"width":391,"height":440,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"263fe36f04f59021","type":"text","text":"# Virtual Levels\nYou haven't mentioned the difference from PL in that reflection is the absorption into a virtual level and instantaneous reemission. Elaborate on that please.","x":-3031,"y":677,"width":296,"height":221,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"a7c91e5fd43b2f6a","type":"text","text":"# Answer: Microscopic Specularity\n\nShort answer: for an **ideal, infinite, perfectly planar interface**, off-specular reflection is exactly zero. It is not \"usually specular\"; it is a symmetry selection rule.[^1][^2]\n\nMicroscopically, for a laterally homogeneous interface, the scattering matrix is diagonal in frequency and in-plane momentum:\n$$\nS(\\mathbf{k}'_{\\parallel},\\omega';\\mathbf{k}_{\\parallel},\\omega)\\propto\n\\delta(\\omega'-\\omega)\\,\\delta^{(2)}(\\mathbf{k}'_{\\parallel}-\\mathbf{k}_{\\parallel}).\n$$\nSo only channels with conserved $\\omega$ and $\\mathbf{k}_{\\parallel}$ have nonzero amplitude. That gives the specular law $\\theta_r=\\theta_i$.\n\nAt the single-photon level this is the same beamsplitter-style result:\n$$\n|1_{\\mathrm{in}}\\rangle \\to r\\,|1_{\\mathrm{spec}}\\rangle + t\\,|1_{\\mathrm{trans}}\\rangle,\n$$\nwith probabilities $P_{\\mathrm{spec}}=|r|^2$, $P_{\\mathrm{trans}}=|t|^2$, and (for the ideal interface) no other angular output modes.[^2]\n\n## Why an off-specular detector can still click in practice\n\nReal experiments are never the ideal limit. Off-specular counts come from channels that break the strict delta-function selection:\n\n1. Finite beam size and finite aperture (angular spectrum / diffraction):\nA real beam has nonzero angular spread, so a slightly shifted detector can intercept the tail of the reflected angular profile.[^3]\n\n2. Surface roughness and defects:\nRoughness breaks perfect in-plane translational symmetry, redistributing power into diffuse/off-specular directions.[^4][^5]\nA common smooth-surface-limit estimate is\n$$\nR_{\\mathrm{spec}}\\approx R_0\\exp\\!\\left[-\\left(\\frac{4\\pi\\sigma\\cos\\theta_i}{\\lambda}\\right)^2\\right],\n$$\nwhere $\\sigma$ is RMS roughness.[^4]\n\n3. Bulk inhomogeneity / contamination / multilayer disorder:\nThese add additional elastic scattering channels.[^1][^5]\n\n4. Inelastic scattering channels (Raman, Brillouin, etc.):\nThen $\\omega'\\neq\\omega$, so those photons are not strictly part of elastic mirror reflection.[^2]\n\n## Direct answer to your thought experiment\n\nIf you illuminate a high-quality mirror with a well-collimated beam and place a detector slightly away from specular:\n- In the ideal theory: expected rate is exactly zero.\n- In real optics: you may detect occasional photons, but those events come from beam divergence + roughness/imperfections (or inelastic background), not from a fundamental probabilistic violation of specular reflection.\n\nSo your intuition is close, with one refinement: it is not that a perfect mirror gives a small intrinsic non-specular probability; rather, **non-specular probability is generated by non-idealities of the field/interface system**.\n\nSupplemental internal anchors: [[2.1 - General Emission Integral]], [[1 - Introduction]], [[A9 - Kirchhoff's Law Of Radiation]].\n\n[^1]: M. Born and E. Wolf, *Principles of Optics* (7th ed., Cambridge University Press). [https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/9781139644181/type/book](https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/9781139644181/type/book)\n[^2]: R. Loudon, *The Quantum Theory of Light* (3rd ed., Oxford University Press, 2000). [https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780198501770.001.0001](https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780198501770.001.0001)\n[^3]: J. W. Goodman, *Introduction to Fourier Optics* (3rd ed., Roberts & Company, 2005). [https://www.macmillanlearning.com/college/us/product/Introduction-to-Fourier-Optics/p/0974707724](https://www.macmillanlearning.com/college/us/product/Introduction-to-Fourier-Optics/p/0974707724)\n[^4]: H. E. Bennett and J. O. Porteus, *Relation Between Surface Roughness and Specular Reflectance at Normal Incidence*, JOSA **51** (1961). [https://doi.org/10.1364/JOSA.51.000123](https://doi.org/10.1364/JOSA.51.000123)\n[^5]: K. E. Torrance and E. M. Sparrow, *Theory for Off-Specular Reflection From Roughened Surfaces*, JOSA **57** (1967). [https://doi.org/10.1364/JOSA.57.001105](https://doi.org/10.1364/JOSA.57.001105)\n","x":-3160,"y":1962,"width":760,"height":1220},
		{"id":"c3e5a94b7d218f60","type":"text","text":"# Answer 2.1\n\nGreat question. If PL is treated as a **transition-rate problem** (FGR over electronic initial/final states), then reflection is treated most naturally as a **driven linear-response scattering problem** of the electronic many-body system.[^1][^2]\n\nAt microscopic level, both start from light-matter coupling (e.g. minimal coupling in Coulomb gauge), but the observable differs:\n- PL: incoherent spontaneous/stimulated emission rate into photon modes.\n- Reflection: coherent, mostly elastic reradiation of the driven polarization/current at the interface.[^3][^4]\n\n## Microscopic route used for reflection\n\nIn linear response, the induced current is\n$$\nJ_i(\\mathbf{q},\\omega)=\\sum_j \\sigma_{ij}(\\mathbf{q},\\omega)\\,E_j(\\mathbf{q},\\omega),\n$$\nwith conductivity from the retarded current-current correlator (Kubo):\n$$\n\\sigma_{ij}(\\mathbf{q},\\omega)=\\frac{i}{\\omega+i0^+}\\Big[\\Pi^{R}_{ij}(\\mathbf{q},\\omega)-\\Pi^{R}_{ij}(\\mathbf{q},0)\\Big].\n$$\nEquivalent dielectric description:\n$$\n\\varepsilon_{ij}(\\mathbf{q},\\omega)=\\delta_{ij}+\\frac{i}{\\varepsilon_0\\omega}\\,\\sigma_{ij}(\\mathbf{q},\\omega).\n$$\nSo the microscopic many-body physics is packed into $\\Pi^R$ (or $\\sigma$), and reflection is then obtained by solving Maxwell boundary conditions with that constitutive response.[^1][^2][^3]\n\nFor an isotropic local medium at normal incidence (vacuum $\\to$ metal):\n$$\nr(\\omega)=\\frac{1-\\tilde n(\\omega)}{1+\\tilde n(\\omega)},\\qquad \\tilde n=\\sqrt{\\varepsilon(\\omega)},\\qquad R=|r|^2.\n$$\nThis is the operational bridge from microscopic electronic dynamics to measured reflectance.[^3][^4]\n\n## Connection to the metal model you already use\n\nA common decomposition is\n$$\n\\varepsilon(\\omega)=\\varepsilon_\\infty-\\frac{\\omega_p^2}{\\omega(\\omega+i\\gamma)}+\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{inter}}(\\omega),\n$$\nwhere Drude captures intraband carriers and $\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{inter}}$ captures bound/interband transitions.[^3]\nFor Au, these optical constants are often benchmarked against Johnson-Christy data.[^5]\n\n## \"FGR-like\" interpretation (to mirror PL intuition)\n\nYou can also view reflection as a photon-mode scattering process with conserved frequency in the elastic channel:\n$$\nW_{i\\to f}=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\hbar}|T_{fi}|^2\\,\\delta(\\hbar\\omega_f-\\hbar\\omega_i),\n$$\nwith\n$$\nT_{fi}\\sim \\sum_n \\frac{\\langle f|H_{\\mathrm{int}}|n\\rangle\\langle n|H_{\\mathrm{int}}|i\\rangle}{E_i-E_n+i0^+}.\n$$\nIn bulk reflection, coherent summation over many electrons/lattice sites enforces specular momentum selection (parallel momentum conserved), which is why reflection is phase-coherent and directional, unlike most PL backgrounds.[^2][^4]\n\n## Practical recipe (parallel to your PL workflow)\n\n1. Compute/fit $\\sigma(\\omega)$ (or $\\varepsilon(\\omega)$) from a microscopic model (Kubo/Kubo-Greenwood).\n2. Convert to $\\varepsilon(\\omega)$ and obtain $\\tilde n(\\omega)$.\n3. Use Fresnel coefficients to get $R(\\omega)$.\n4. In experiments, separate elastic reflected line at $\\omega_L$ from inelastic PL continuum in post-processing.\n\nSupplemental internal anchors: [[2.1 - General Emission Integral]], [[A9 - Kirchhoff's Law Of Radiation]], [[1 - Introduction]].\n\n[^1]: R. Kubo, *Statistical-Mechanical Theory of Irreversible Processes. I*, J. Phys. Soc. Jpn. **12**, 570 (1957). [https://doi.org/10.1143/JPSJ.12.570](https://doi.org/10.1143/JPSJ.12.570)\n[^2]: G. D. Mahan, *Many-Particle Physics* (3rd ed., 2000). [https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-5714-9](https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-5714-9)\n[^3]: M. Dressel and G. Gruner, *Electrodynamics of Solids* (Cambridge Univ. Press, 2002). [https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511606168](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511606168)\n[^4]: M. Born and E. Wolf, *Principles of Optics* (7th ed.). [https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108769914](https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108769914)\n[^5]: P. B. Johnson and R. W. Christy, *Optical Constants of the Noble Metals*, Phys. Rev. B **6**, 4370 (1972). [https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.6.4370](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.6.4370)\n","x":-2090,"y":371,"width":760,"height":1260}
	],
